  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, expect widely scattered off-and-on rain\nshowers to continue into this evening before slowly ending from\nwest to east across the Northland. Gusty northwest winds will \nalso reach up to 30 mph early this afternoon, but these winds \nwill begin decreasing late this afternoon and into the evening \nhours.\n\nAs we move into Thursday and Friday, dry weather will dominate\nfor nearly all of our region. However, a few more localized and\nbrief rain showers are forecast for the tip of the Arrowhead on\nThursday afternoon. As skies clear and light winds become calm,\na chilly night is forecast Thursday night, cold spots will \nreach the low 40s and pockets dropping into the upper 30s by \nearly Friday morning.\n\nBy late Friday night into Saturday morning, an incoming warm air\nadvection regime will set up and bring our next round of\nthunderstorm chances. These periodic thunderstorm chances will\nlast through much of the weekend as low pressure traverses the\nNorthern Plains. Strong thunderstorms are possible this weekend,\ndepending on exactly where the warm front sets up over the area.\n\nCurrent trends keep the best potential for strong storms along\nand south of the Highway 2 corridor, particularly from the\nBrainerd Lakes to the Interstate 35 corridor and into inland\nnorthwest Wisconsin. As the low pressure passes through the \nUpper Midwest Sunday PM into Monday daytime, there is a slightly\nbetter environment progged in medium range global guidance for \nstrong thunderstorms to maybe even scrape the levels of severe \nweather. If strong thunderstorms can form this weekend, Sunday \nPM is most likely across much of the region south of US Hwy 2 \nand Monday being far eastern Minnesota into much of northwest \nWisconsin. Drier and quieter conditions are favored to return by\nnext Tuesday.\n\nA slightly warmer airmass arrives this weekend and persist into\nearly next week. Temperatures on Sunday are forecast to reach \nthe mid 80s, and if the warmer guidance verifies, some spots in \nthe warm sector could even scrape the upper 80s. However, the \nexact location of weekend storms and lingering sky cover will \nheavily influence whether these warmer temperatures are \nrealized.",
